00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:20.250
So this is to say that with programming you have the ability in like 10 lines of text,

00:12:20.429 --> 00:12:21.940
not all of which you might understand yet,

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:25.849
but that's the whole point of a class like this to build fairly powerful things,

00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:27.330
maybe silly things like this,

00:12:27.419 --> 00:12:30.500
but in fact it's using the same primitives that CS 50 has its own.

00:12:30.856 --> 00:12:33.455
Virtual rubber duck and we'll talk more about this in the weeks to come,

00:12:33.495 --> 00:12:34.375
but long story short,

00:12:34.536 --> 00:12:35.705
in the world of programming,

00:12:35.736 --> 00:12:36.645
it's kind of a thing

00:12:36.815 --> 00:12:40.776
to keep a rubber duck literally on your desk or really any inanimate

00:12:40.776 --> 00:12:44.276
cute object like this because when you are struggling with some problems,

00:12:44.416 --> 00:12:47.325
some bug or mistake in your code and you don't have a friend,

00:12:47.375 --> 00:12:48.335
a teaching assistant,

00:12:48.416 --> 00:12:48.906
a parent,

00:12:48.916 --> 00:12:50.875
or someone else who's more knowledgeable than you.

00:12:50.932 --> 00:12:53.521
About code where you literally are encouraged in programming

00:12:53.521 --> 00:12:56.202
circles to like talk to the rubber duck,

00:12:56.211 --> 00:12:59.192
and it's through that process of just verbalizing your confusion

00:12:59.361 --> 00:13:03.242
and organizing your thoughts enough to convey it to another person or duck in

00:13:03.242 --> 00:13:06.161
this case that so often that proverbial light bulb goes off and you realize,

00:13:06.281 --> 00:13:06.351
ah,

00:13:06.682 --> 00:13:11.081
I'm being an idiot now I hear in my own thoughts the illogic or the mistake I'm making.

00:13:11.390 --> 00:13:13.440
And you solve that problem as well.

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:14.289
So CS 50,

00:13:14.369 --> 00:13:15.369
drawing inspiration from this,

00:13:15.409 --> 00:13:18.599
will give to you a virtual duck in computer form.

00:13:18.729 --> 00:13:19.090
And in fact,

00:13:19.210 --> 00:13:22.289
among the other URLs you'll use over the course of the semester is that here,

00:13:22.369 --> 00:13:23.570
CS50.AI,

00:13:23.770 --> 00:13:25.960
which is also built into that previous URL,

00:13:26.140 --> 00:13:26.690
CS50.

00:13:26.859 --> 00:13:27.210
dev,

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:31.010
whereby these are the AIs you can use in CS50

00:13:31.010 --> 00:13:33.359
to solve problems and you are encouraged to do so.

00:13:33.609 --> 00:13:34.809
As you'll see in the course of syllabus,

00:13:34.849 --> 00:13:35.919
it is not reasonable.

00:13:36.271 --> 00:13:39.312
Not allowed to use AI-based software other than CS 50's own,

00:13:39.392 --> 00:13:39.971
be it Claw,

00:13:40.062 --> 00:13:40.341
Gemini,

00:13:40.581 --> 00:13:41.132
Chachi PT,

00:13:41.142 --> 00:13:41.581
or the like,

00:13:41.661 --> 00:13:44.541
but it is reasonable and very much encouraged along the

00:13:44.541 --> 00:13:46.622
way to turn not only to humans like me,

00:13:46.742 --> 00:13:47.892
your teaching assistant,

00:13:48.101 --> 00:13:49.171
and others in the class,

00:13:49.262 --> 00:13:51.351
but to CS 50's own AI-based software.

00:13:51.421 --> 00:13:56.541
And what you'll find is that this virtual duck is designed to behave as close to a good

00:13:56.541 --> 00:14:00.981
human tutor as you might expect from an actual human in the real world knows about CS.

00:14:01.354 --> 00:14:02.984
knows how to lead you to a solution,

00:14:03.193 --> 00:14:07.064
ideally without simply spoiling it and providing it outright.

00:14:07.794 --> 00:14:08.903
So with that said,

00:14:08.994 --> 00:14:12.473
that's sort of the end game to be able to write code like that and more.

00:14:12.593 --> 00:14:16.213
But let's really start back at the beginning and see how we can't get

00:14:16.513 --> 00:14:18.684
from zeros and ones that computers speak

00:14:18.684 --> 00:14:21.033
all the way back to artificial intelligence.

00:14:21.073 --> 00:14:23.914
So computer science is in the name of the course,

00:14:24.153 --> 00:14:24.914
Computer Science 50,

00:14:24.953 --> 00:14:25.473
but what is that?

00:14:25.643 --> 00:14:25.674
Well,

00:14:25.684 --> 00:14:27.366
it's really Just the study of information.

00:14:27.565 --> 00:14:28.835
How do you represent it?

00:14:29.046 --> 00:14:29.966
How do you process it?

00:14:29.995 --> 00:14:31.526
and very much germane to computer science

00:14:31.526 --> 00:14:33.406
is what the world calls computational thinking,

00:14:33.486 --> 00:14:36.716
which is just the application of ideas from computer science,

00:14:36.885 --> 00:14:40.476
or CS to problems generally in the real world.

00:14:40.486 --> 00:14:41.606
And in fact that's ultimately,

00:14:41.645 --> 00:14:44.166
I dare say what computer science really is.

00:14:44.325 --> 00:14:47.046
It's about problem solving and even though we use computers,

00:14:47.205 --> 00:14:48.885
you learn how to program along the way,

00:14:48.926 --> 00:14:50.846
these are really just tools and methodologies.

00:14:51.338 --> 00:14:53.857
that you can leverage to solve problems.

00:14:53.937 --> 00:14:54.888
Now what does that mean?

00:14:55.057 --> 00:14:55.138
Well,

00:14:55.218 --> 00:14:59.127
a problem is perhaps most easily distilled into a simple picture like this.

00:14:59.297 --> 00:15:00.148
We've got some input,

00:15:00.218 --> 00:15:01.728
which is like the problem we want to solve,

00:15:01.898 --> 00:15:02.607
and the output,

00:15:02.687 --> 00:15:04.968
which is the goal we want the solution there too.

00:15:05.047 --> 00:15:07.528
And then somewhere in the middle here is the proverbial black box,

00:15:07.617 --> 00:15:09.728
the sort of secret sauce that gets that input

00:15:10.088 --> 00:15:10.737
from output.

00:15:10.857 --> 00:15:14.038
So this then I would say is in essence is problem solving

00:15:14.218 --> 00:15:15.528
and thus computer science.

00:15:16.109 --> 00:15:17.169
But we have to agree,

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:18.609
especially if we're going to use devices,

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:18.940
Macs,

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:19.409
PCs,

00:15:19.450 --> 00:15:19.609
phones,

00:15:19.690 --> 00:15:20.140
whatever,

00:15:20.330 --> 00:15:22.169
how do we all represent information,

00:15:22.250 --> 00:15:24.969
the inputs and the outputs in some standardized way?

00:15:25.070 --> 00:15:25.849
Is it with English?

00:15:25.950 --> 00:15:26.700
Is it with something else?

00:15:26.809 --> 00:15:26.890
Well,

00:15:26.969 --> 00:15:27.700
you all probably know,

00:15:27.770 --> 00:15:29.200
even if you're not computer people,

00:15:29.289 --> 00:15:30.359
that at the end of the day

00:15:30.770 --> 00:15:33.530
computers somehow use zeros in one entirely.

00:15:33.614 --> 00:15:35.325
That is their entire alphabet.

00:15:35.515 --> 00:15:36.275
And in fact

00:15:36.565 --> 00:15:39.085
you might be familiar already with certain such systems.

00:15:39.164 --> 00:15:41.205
So the unary notation,

00:15:41.364 --> 00:15:44.804
which means you essentially use single digits like fingers on your hand,

00:15:44.885 --> 00:15:45.205
for instance,

00:15:45.325 --> 00:15:46.835
unary AKA base one,

00:15:46.844 --> 00:15:48.405
is something you can do on your own human hand.

00:15:48.484 --> 00:15:49.114
So for instance,

00:15:49.244 --> 00:15:49.965
with one human hand,

00:15:50.044 --> 00:15:50.965
how high can I count?

00:15:52.309 --> 00:15:52.429
All right,

00:15:52.469 --> 00:15:54.820
so hopefully 12345,

00:15:54.909 --> 00:15:59.409
and if you want to count to 6 to 11 and 10 and so forth,

00:15:59.500 --> 00:15:59.969
you need to,

00:16:00.070 --> 00:16:00.200
you know,

00:16:00.309 --> 00:16:03.349
take out another hand or your toes or the like because it's fairly limiting.

00:16:03.390 --> 00:16:06.200
But if I think a little harder instead of just using unary,

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:08.900
what if I use a different system instead?

00:16:09.229 --> 00:16:10.469
What about something like binary?

00:16:10.549 --> 00:16:10.630
Well,

00:16:10.719 --> 00:16:12.289
how high if you think a little harder,

00:16:12.309 --> 00:16:13.859
can you count on one human hand?

00:16:15.099 --> 00:16:16.109
So 31,

00:16:16.229 --> 00:16:17.989
says someone who studied computer science before,

00:16:18.039 --> 00:16:18.880
but why is that?

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:20.070
It's kind of hard to imagine,

00:16:20.239 --> 00:16:20.510
right?

00:16:20.799 --> 00:16:24.799
Because 12345 seems to be the five possible patterns,

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:27.119
but that's only when you're looking at the totality of fingers

00:16:27.119 --> 00:16:29.640
that are actually up 5 in total or 4 in total,

00:16:29.650 --> 00:16:30.830
or 1 or the like.

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:33.719
But what if we take into account the pattern of fingers that

00:16:33.719 --> 00:16:36.890
are up and we just standardize what each of those fingers represent?

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:38.000
So maybe we all agree.

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:39.859
Like a good computer would too,

00:16:40.010 --> 00:16:42.539
that maybe no fingers up means the number 0.

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:43.869
And if we want to count to 1,

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:45.119
let's go with the obvious.

00:16:45.289 --> 00:16:46.320
This is now 1.

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:48.169
But instead of 2 being this,

00:16:48.289 --> 00:16:49.640
which was my first instinct,

00:16:49.809 --> 00:16:51.599
maybe 2 can just be

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:52.359
this

00:16:52.570 --> 00:16:55.400
a single second finger up like this,

00:16:55.650 --> 00:17:01.010
and that means we could now use 2 fingers up to represent 3.

00:17:01.161 --> 00:17:06.472
I propose we can use just one middle finger up to offend everyone but represent 4.

00:17:06.671 --> 00:17:13.261
I could maybe use these two fingers with some difficulty to represent 567.

00:17:13.321 --> 00:17:16.261
I'm already up to 7 having used only 3 fingers.

00:17:16.281 --> 00:17:16.671
And in fact,

00:17:16.712 --> 00:17:18.381
if we keep going higher and higher,

00:17:18.472 --> 00:17:23.001
I bet I can get as high as 31 for 32 possible combinations,

00:17:23.071 --> 00:17:23.871
but the first one was.

00:17:24.053 --> 00:17:25.874
So that's as high as we can count.

00:17:25.973 --> 00:17:27.773
So we'll make this connection in just a moment,

00:17:27.854 --> 00:17:30.583
but what I started to do there is something called base 2.

00:17:30.744 --> 00:17:33.004
Instead of just having fingers up or fingers down,

00:17:33.173 --> 00:17:35.933
I'm taking into account the positions of those fingers

00:17:35.933 --> 00:17:39.014
and giving meaning to like this finger here,

00:17:39.213 --> 00:17:39.854
this finger here,

00:17:39.933 --> 00:17:40.723
this finger here,

00:17:40.764 --> 00:17:41.213
and so forth,

00:17:41.254 --> 00:17:42.764
different weights if you will.

00:17:43.093 --> 00:17:43.404
So

00:17:43.534 --> 00:17:46.654
the binary system is indeed all computers.

00:17:46.806 --> 00:17:49.755
And you might be familiar with some terminology here.

00:17:49.875 --> 00:17:52.265
Binary digit is not really something anyone really says,

00:17:52.475 --> 00:17:54.865
but the shorthand for that is going to be

00:17:55.235 --> 00:17:55.556
bit.

00:17:55.836 --> 00:17:57.076
So if you've heard of bits,

00:17:57.105 --> 00:17:59.355
and we'll soon see bytes and then kilobytes

00:17:59.355 --> 00:18:02.026
and megabytes and gigabytes and terabytes and more,

00:18:02.235 --> 00:18:05.745
this just refers to a bit meaning a single

00:18:05.875 --> 00:18:07.196
binary digit,

00:18:07.235 --> 00:18:09.475
either a 0 or a 1.

00:18:10.089 --> 00:18:13.920
A 0 is perhaps most simply represented by just like turning,

00:18:14.050 --> 00:18:15.359
maybe keeping a finger down,

00:18:15.489 --> 00:18:18.369
or in the world of computers which have access to electricity,

00:18:18.449 --> 00:18:19.479
be it from the wall

00:18:19.849 --> 00:18:21.239
or maybe a battery,

00:18:21.369 --> 00:18:22.589
you know what we could do?

00:18:23.050 --> 00:18:25.829
We could just decide sort of universally that when

00:18:26.050 --> 00:18:27.359
a light bulb is off,

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:28.849
that thing represents a zero,

00:18:28.859 --> 00:18:29.650
and when the light bulb.

00:18:29.755 --> 00:18:32.994
Was on that thing's going to represent a 1 instead.

00:18:33.244 --> 00:18:34.084
Now why is this?

00:18:34.204 --> 00:18:34.244
Well,

00:18:34.525 --> 00:18:35.724
electricity is such a simple thing,

00:18:35.824 --> 00:18:35.885
right?

00:18:35.925 --> 00:18:38.114
It's either flowing or it's not,

00:18:38.285 --> 00:18:41.305
and we don't even have to therefore worry about how much of it is flowing.

00:18:41.425 --> 00:18:43.915
If you're vaguely remember a little bit about voltage,

00:18:43.925 --> 00:18:45.675
we can sort of be like zero volts.

00:18:45.844 --> 00:18:47.045
Nothing's there available for us,

00:18:47.055 --> 00:18:49.444
or maybe it's 5 volts or something else in between.

00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:54.939
But what's nice about binary only housing zeros and ones is that it maps really

00:18:54.939 --> 00:18:58.290
nicely to the real world by like throwing a light switch on and off.

00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:00.420
You can represent information by just using a

00:19:00.420 --> 00:19:02.819
little bit of electricity or the lack thereof.

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:04.229
So what do I mean by this?

00:19:04.380 --> 00:19:04.500
Well,

00:19:04.770 --> 00:19:09.660
suppose we want to start counting using binary zeros and ones only.

00:19:09.739 --> 00:19:09.810
Well,

00:19:09.819 --> 00:19:12.180
let's think of them metaphorically as like akin to these light.

00:19:12.296 --> 00:19:15.446
Bulbs here and in fact let me grab a few of these light bulbs

00:19:15.446 --> 00:19:18.475
and let me propose that if we want to represent the number 0,

00:19:18.725 --> 00:19:18.885
well,

00:19:18.926 --> 00:19:21.115
it stands to reason that here

00:19:21.566 --> 00:19:25.595
single light bulb that is off can be agreed upon as representing 0.

00:19:25.765 --> 00:19:26.526
Now in practice,

00:19:26.965 --> 00:19:28.645
computers don't have little light bulbs inside,

00:19:28.686 --> 00:19:31.235
but they do have little switches inside,

00:19:31.485 --> 00:19:34.686
millions of tiny little things called transistors that have.

00:19:34.842 --> 00:19:37.832
On can allow it to capture a little bit of electricity and

00:19:37.832 --> 00:19:41.271
effectively turn on a metaphorical bulb or the switch can go off.

00:19:41.432 --> 00:19:43.232
The transistor can go off and therefore let the

00:19:43.232 --> 00:19:46.271
electricity dissipate and you have just now a 0.

00:19:46.592 --> 00:19:47.421
Unfortunately,

00:19:47.592 --> 00:19:49.222
even though I can let some

00:19:49.692 --> 00:19:50.501
electricity,

00:19:51.151 --> 00:19:53.381
here's the battery I mentioned is required,

00:19:53.671 --> 00:19:55.911
even though we might have some electricity available to us,

00:19:55.952 --> 00:19:57.342
I can therefore count to 1.

00:19:57.671 --> 00:19:59.151
But how do I go about counting?

00:20:01.510 --> 00:20:02.290
Hardware problem,

00:20:02.390 --> 00:20:04.699
how do I go about counting higher than one

00:20:04.949 --> 00:20:06.540
with just a light bulb?

00:20:08.390 --> 00:20:08.579
Yeah,

00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:09.380
so I need more of them.

00:20:09.459 --> 00:20:11.250
So let me grab another one here,

00:20:11.339 --> 00:20:13.170
and now I can put it next to it,

00:20:13.219 --> 00:20:16.050
and this too I'll claim is just still the number one.

00:20:16.180 --> 00:20:17.930
But if I want to turn 2 of them on,

00:20:18.099 --> 00:20:18.400
well,

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:20.140
that would mean I could count to 2.

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:23.550
And if I maybe grab another one now I can count as high as 3,

00:20:23.579 --> 00:20:24.369
but wait a minute,

00:20:24.500 --> 00:20:25.680
I'm doing something wrong

00:20:26.069 --> 00:20:27.219
because with 3 human fingers,

00:20:27.310 --> 00:20:28.650
how high was they able to count.

00:20:29.939 --> 00:20:31.829
So 7 in total starting at 0.

00:20:31.979 --> 00:20:33.339
So I've done something wrong here,

00:20:33.380 --> 00:20:36.930
but let me be a little more clever than about the pattern that I'm actually using.

00:20:37.099 --> 00:20:38.650
Perhaps this can still be

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:39.410
1,

00:20:39.619 --> 00:20:44.770
but just like my finger went up and only one finger in the second version of this,

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:45.839
this

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:48.140
can be what we represent as 2.

00:20:49.010 --> 00:20:50.729
Which one do I want to turn on as 3,

00:20:50.780 --> 00:20:52.119
your left or your right?

00:20:53.069 --> 00:20:53.920
So you're right,

00:20:53.959 --> 00:20:56.579
because now this matches what I was doing with my fingers a moment ago,

00:20:56.599 --> 00:20:59.030
and I claimed we could represent 3 like this.

00:20:59.160 --> 00:21:00.189
If we want to represent 4,

00:21:00.199 --> 00:21:00.719
that's fine.

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:02.109
We have to turn that off,

00:21:02.439 --> 00:21:03.270
this off,

00:21:03.520 --> 00:21:05.040
and this on,

00:21:05.359 --> 00:21:06.750
and that's somehow 4

00:21:06.920 --> 00:21:08.199
and let's go all the way up to 7.

00:21:08.250 --> 00:21:10.640
Which ones need to be on to represent the number 7?

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:11.520
All right,

00:21:11.640 --> 00:21:13.030
so all of them here.

00:21:13.199 --> 00:21:15.949
Now if you're not among those who just sort of naturally said

00:21:16.199 --> 00:21:16.599
all of them,

00:21:16.619 --> 00:21:17.839
like what the heck is going on?

00:21:17.920 --> 00:21:21.439
How do half the people in this room know what these patterns are supposed to be?

00:21:21.599 --> 00:21:21.640
Well,

00:21:21.719 --> 00:21:23.469
maybe you're remembering what I did with my fingers,

00:21:23.640 --> 00:21:25.569
but it turns out you're already pretty familiar

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:27.310
with systems like this,

00:21:27.319 --> 00:21:29.150
even if you might not have put a name to it.

00:21:29.319 --> 00:21:30.180
So in the human world,

00:21:30.239 --> 00:21:30.839
the real world,

00:21:30.920 --> 00:21:32.589
most of us deal every day with

00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:34.520
the so-called base 10 system,

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:35.329
otherwise known as.

00:21:35.890 --> 00:21:41.250
deck implying 10 because in the decimal system you have 10 digits available to you 0

00:21:41.660 --> 00:21:42.369
through 9.

00:21:42.459 --> 00:21:45.449
In the binary system we only had 2 by implying 2,

00:21:45.660 --> 00:21:47.010
so 0 and 1.

00:21:47.140 --> 00:21:48.869
And unary we had just 1,

00:21:48.979 --> 00:21:51.410
a single digit there or not.

00:21:51.619 --> 00:21:55.489
So in the decimal system we just have more of a vocabulary to play with,

00:21:55.500 --> 00:21:57.699
and yet you and I have been doing this since grade school.

00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:00.020
So this is obviously the number 123.

00:22:00.520 --> 00:22:01.170
But why?

00:22:01.250 --> 00:22:03.969
It's technically just three symbols 123,

00:22:04.140 --> 00:22:04.780
but most of us,

00:22:04.859 --> 00:22:05.839
your mind immediately goes,

00:22:05.900 --> 00:22:06.109
OK,

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:07.329
123,

00:22:07.420 --> 00:22:08.020
pretty obvious,

00:22:08.099 --> 00:22:08.890
pretty natural.

00:22:09.099 --> 00:22:10.380
But at some point you,

00:22:10.459 --> 00:22:10.819
like me,

00:22:10.900 --> 00:22:13.329
were probably taught that this is the one's place

00:22:13.540 --> 00:22:18.130
and this is the ten's place and this is the 100s place and so forth.

00:22:18.209 --> 00:22:21.359
And the reason that this pattern of symbols

00:22:21.699 --> 00:22:26.020
123 is 123 is that we're all doing some quick mental math and.

00:22:26.340 --> 00:22:26.380
I,

00:22:26.500 --> 00:22:26.571
well,

00:22:26.581 --> 00:22:30.500
that's 100 times 1 plus 10 times 2 plus 1 times 30,

00:22:30.620 --> 00:22:30.821
OK,

00:22:30.901 --> 00:22:33.500
there's how we get 100 plus 20 + 3 gives

00:22:33.500 --> 00:22:36.931
us the number we all know mathematically is 123.

00:22:37.140 --> 00:22:37.220
Well,

00:22:37.260 --> 00:22:41.020
it turns out whether you're using decimal or binary or other

00:22:41.020 --> 00:22:43.171
base systems that we'll talk about later in the course,

00:22:43.250 --> 00:22:45.090
the system is still fundamentally the same.

00:22:45.140 --> 00:22:46.541
Let's kind of generalize this away.

00:22:46.661 --> 00:22:49.461
Here's a three digit number in some base system,

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:50.421
specifically in decimal,

00:22:50.431 --> 00:22:51.821
and I know that only because of

00:22:51.982 --> 00:22:54.712
Placeholders that I've got on top of each of these numbers.

00:22:54.781 --> 00:22:56.332
But if we do a little bit of math here,

00:22:56.491 --> 00:22:56.921
1,

00:22:56.932 --> 00:22:57.281
10,

00:22:57.381 --> 00:22:57.982
100,

00:22:58.141 --> 00:22:58.741
1000,

00:22:58.751 --> 00:22:59.342
10,000,

00:22:59.352 --> 00:22:59.781
and so forth,

00:22:59.832 --> 00:23:00.682
what's the pattern?

00:23:00.692 --> 00:23:00.702
Well,

00:23:00.991 --> 00:23:02.822
technically this is 100,

00:23:02.921 --> 00:23:03.751
10 to 1,

00:23:03.832 --> 00:23:04.891
10 to 2,

00:23:05.102 --> 00:23:06.052
and so forth,

00:23:06.222 --> 00:23:11.822
and we're using 10 because we can use as many as 10 digits under each of those columns.

00:23:11.862 --> 00:23:15.852
But if we take some of those digits away and go from decimal down to binary,

00:23:16.062 --> 00:23:18.542
the motivation being it's Way easier for

00:23:18.542 --> 00:23:22.103
a computer to distinguish electricity being on or

00:23:22.103 --> 00:23:26.623
off than coming up with like 10 unique levels of electricity to distinguish among.

00:23:26.703 --> 00:23:27.453
You could do it.

00:23:27.623 --> 00:23:30.493
It would be annoying and difficult to build and hardware you could do it

00:23:30.863 --> 00:23:32.652
so much simpler to just say

00:23:32.942 --> 00:23:34.223
on and off.

00:23:34.302 --> 00:23:36.233
It's a nice simple world that way.

00:23:36.383 --> 00:23:37.692
So let's change the base

00:23:38.192 --> 00:23:39.292
from 10 to 2,

00:23:39.503 --> 00:23:40.463
and what does this get us?

00:23:40.503 --> 00:23:40.583
Well,

00:23:40.623 --> 00:23:41.953
if we now do undo the math,

00:23:42.103 --> 00:23:43.223
that's 2 to 0.

00:23:43.543 --> 00:23:46.654
121 is 222 is 4.

00:23:46.943 --> 00:23:49.734
So the the mental math is now about to be the same,

00:23:49.984 --> 00:23:53.034
but the columns represent something a little bit different.

00:23:53.144 --> 00:23:53.784
So for instance,

00:23:53.823 --> 00:23:55.904
if I turn all of these off again,

00:23:56.343 --> 00:23:57.494
such that I've got off,

00:23:57.543 --> 00:23:57.793
off,

00:23:57.864 --> 00:23:58.374
off,

00:23:58.583 --> 00:24:01.254
otherwise known as 000,

00:24:01.264 --> 00:24:08.183
it's 0 because it's 4 times 0 + 2 times 0 + 1 times 0 still gives me 0.

00:24:08.343 --> 00:24:08.943
By contrast,

00:24:09.354 --> 00:24:13.214
If I turn on maybe just this one all the way over on the left,

00:24:13.464 --> 00:24:13.655
well,

00:24:13.734 --> 00:24:15.905
that's 4 times 1

00:24:16.344 --> 00:24:18.814
because on represents 1 and off represents 0,

00:24:18.895 --> 00:24:21.035
plus 2 times 0 plus 1 times 0,

00:24:21.145 --> 00:24:22.214
that gives me 4.

00:24:22.295 --> 00:24:24.125
And if I turn both of these on

00:24:24.425 --> 00:24:26.834
such that all three of them are now on,

00:24:27.064 --> 00:24:27.265
on,

00:24:27.295 --> 00:24:27.505
on,

00:24:27.584 --> 00:24:27.925
on,

00:24:27.984 --> 00:24:30.385
AKA 111,

00:24:30.395 --> 00:24:34.714
that's 4 times 1 plus 2 times 1 plus 1 times 1.

00:24:35.040 --> 00:24:36.319
That then gives me 7,

00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:38.479
and we can keep adding more and more bits to this.

00:24:38.520 --> 00:24:38.719
In fact,

00:24:38.800 --> 00:24:39.959
if we go all the way up

00:24:40.290 --> 00:24:40.930
numerically,

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:45.150
here's how we would represent in binary the number you and I know as 0.

00:24:45.339 --> 00:24:46.650
Here's how we would represent

00:24:47.000 --> 00:24:47.469
1.

00:24:47.709 --> 00:24:49.229
Here's how we would represent 2

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:50.430
and 3

00:24:50.719 --> 00:24:52.469
and 4 and 5,

00:24:52.479 --> 00:24:53.800
and you can kind of see in your mind's eyes.

00:24:53.935 --> 00:24:55.844
Now because I only have zeros and

00:24:55.964 --> 00:24:58.055
ones and no 2s or threes,

00:24:58.324 --> 00:24:59.324
not to mention nines,

00:24:59.405 --> 00:25:02.895
I'm essentially going to be carrying a 1 in a moment if we were to be doing some math.

00:25:02.925 --> 00:25:04.155
So to go from 5

00:25:04.405 --> 00:25:05.234
to 6,

00:25:05.484 --> 00:25:11.314
that's why the 1 ends up in the middle column to go to 7 here gives us now 111 or on on

00:25:11.494 --> 00:25:11.525
on,

00:25:11.564 --> 00:25:12.805
how do I represent 8?

00:25:14.069 --> 00:25:16.180
Using ones and zeros,

00:25:16.310 --> 00:25:16.739
yeah,

00:25:18.469 --> 00:25:18.589
yeah,

00:25:18.750 --> 00:25:20.189
so we're going to need to add another digit.

00:25:20.270 --> 00:25:23.140
We need to throw hardware at the problem using an additional

00:25:23.140 --> 00:25:26.109
digit so that we actually have a column representing 8.

00:25:26.270 --> 00:25:26.380
Now,

00:25:26.390 --> 00:25:27.030
as an aside,

00:25:27.040 --> 00:25:28.420
and we'll talk about this before long,

00:25:28.630 --> 00:25:31.619
if you don't have an additional digit available,

00:25:31.790 --> 00:25:33.550
if your computer doesn't have enough memory,

00:25:33.630 --> 00:25:34.339
so to speak,

00:25:34.510 --> 00:25:38.760
you might accidentally count from 01234567.

00:25:38.989 --> 00:25:40.209
And then accidentally

00:25:40.380 --> 00:25:43.650
end up back at 0 because if there's no room to store

00:25:43.890 --> 00:25:45.099
the 4th bit,

00:25:45.459 --> 00:25:45.619
well,

00:25:45.670 --> 00:25:47.619
all you have is part of the number,

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:52.130
and this is going to create all sorts of problems then ultimately in the real world.

00:25:52.260 --> 00:25:56.130
So let me go ahead and put these back and propose that we have a system now

00:25:56.500 --> 00:26:01.369
if you agree to sort of count numbers in this way via which we can represent

00:26:02.020 --> 00:26:05.180
information in some standard way and all the device underneath the hood.

00:26:05.300 --> 00:26:07.329
It is a bit of electricity to make this work.

00:26:07.369 --> 00:26:08.800
It's got to be able to turn things on,

00:26:08.969 --> 00:26:10.449
AKA use some transistors,

00:26:10.459 --> 00:26:11.890
and it's got to be able to turn those things

00:26:11.890 --> 00:26:15.959
off so as to represent zeros instead of ones.

00:26:16.089 --> 00:26:17.890
But the reality is like 2 bits,

00:26:17.969 --> 00:26:18.449
3 bits,

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:22.790
4 bits aren't very useful in the real world because even with 3 bits you can count to 7,

00:26:22.849 --> 00:26:24.359
with 4 you can count to 15.

00:26:24.569 --> 00:26:25.969
These aren't very big numbers,

00:26:26.040 --> 00:26:31.530
so it tends to be more common to actually use units of measure of 8 bits at a time.

00:26:32.189 --> 00:26:33.510
A byte is just that.

00:26:33.800 --> 00:26:35.359
1 byte is 8 bits.

00:26:35.439 --> 00:26:37.680
So if you've ever used the vernacular of kilobytes,

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:38.369
megabytes,

00:26:38.439 --> 00:26:39.199
gigabytes,

00:26:39.319 --> 00:26:41.680
that's just referring to some number of bits,

00:26:41.719 --> 00:26:42.229
but

00:26:42.760 --> 00:26:43.510
8 of them

00:26:43.920 --> 00:26:46.380
together compose one individual byte.

00:26:46.560 --> 00:26:49.930
So here for instance is a byte worth of bits,

00:26:49.989 --> 00:26:50.989
8 of them total.

00:26:51.119 --> 00:26:53.069
I've added all the additional placeholders,

00:26:53.359 --> 00:26:55.199
and what number does this represent in decimal

00:26:55.199 --> 00:26:57.800
even though you're looking at 8 binary digits.

00:26:58.920 --> 00:26:59.479
It's just 0,

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:02.109
because like literally every column is a 0.

00:27:02.239 --> 00:27:03.869
Now this is a bit more of mental math,

00:27:03.930 --> 00:27:05.270
but unless you know it already,

00:27:05.439 --> 00:27:07.239
what if I change all of the zeros to ones?

00:27:07.250 --> 00:27:08.630
I turn all 8 light bulbs on,

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:09.550
what number is this?

00:27:10.780 --> 00:27:11.089
Yeah,

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:12.449
so 255.

00:27:12.530 --> 00:27:14.969
Now some of those of you who didn't get that instantly,

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:15.410
that's fine.

00:27:15.489 --> 00:27:16.930
You could certainly do the math manually.

00:27:16.969 --> 00:27:20.890
I dare say some of you have some prior knowledge of how to do this sort of system,

00:27:21.010 --> 00:27:23.329
but 255 means

00:27:23.569 --> 00:27:26.949
that if you start counting at 0 and you go all the way up to 255.

00:27:27.005 --> 00:27:27.194
OK,

00:27:27.234 --> 00:27:30.635
that's 256 total possibilities.

00:27:30.714 --> 00:27:34.584
Once you include 0 in the total number of patterns of zeros and ones,

00:27:34.594 --> 00:27:37.864
and this is just going to be one of these common numbers in computer science,

00:27:37.994 --> 00:27:39.224
256.

00:27:39.234 --> 00:27:39.584
Why?

00:27:39.755 --> 00:27:42.114
Because it's referring to 8 of something,

00:27:42.224 --> 00:27:43.114
2 to 8.

00:27:43.640 --> 00:27:47.479
Gives you 256 and so you're going to commonly see certain values like that.

00:27:47.540 --> 00:27:49.020
256 back in the day,

00:27:49.280 --> 00:27:52.599
computers could only show 256 colors on the screen.

00:27:52.680 --> 00:27:55.640
Certain graphics formats nowadays that you might download can

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:58.349
only use as many as 256 colors because,

00:27:58.400 --> 00:27:59.069
as we'll see,

00:27:59.280 --> 00:28:00.310
they're only using,

00:28:00.479 --> 00:28:01.089
for instance,

00:28:01.099 --> 00:28:03.920
8 bits and therefore they can only represent so

00:28:03.920 --> 00:28:06.430
many colors of the rainbow as a result.

00:28:07.209 --> 00:28:07.689
So

00:28:08.050 --> 00:28:08.599
this

00:28:08.930 --> 00:28:11.819
then is how we might go from just zeros and ones,

00:28:11.930 --> 00:28:13.589
electricity inside of a computer

00:28:13.930 --> 00:28:16.400
to storing actual numbers with which we're familiar

00:28:16.400 --> 00:28:18.209
and honestly we can go higher than 255.

00:28:18.290 --> 00:28:20.599
What do you need to count higher than 255?

00:28:20.609 --> 00:28:21.300
1/9 bit,

00:28:21.489 --> 00:28:22.050
a 10th bit,

00:28:22.339 --> 00:28:25.959
11th bit and so forth and it turns out common conventions nowadays,

00:28:26.040 --> 00:28:31.040
and we'll see this in code 2 is to use as many as 32 bits at a time.

00:28:31.160 --> 00:28:34.160
So that's a good chunk of bits and anyone want to ballpark how

00:28:34.160 --> 00:28:37.670
high you can count if you've got 32 bits available to you.

00:28:39.849 --> 00:28:40.079
Oh,

00:28:40.199 --> 00:28:41.069
fewer people now,

00:28:41.239 --> 00:28:41.280
yeah,

00:28:41.359 --> 00:28:41.869
in the back.

00:28:42.790 --> 00:28:43.020
Yeah,

00:28:43.140 --> 00:28:44.510
so it's roughly 4 billion,

00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:48.339
and it's technically 2 billion if you also want to represent negative numbers,

00:28:48.349 --> 00:28:49.729
but we'll revisit that question,

00:28:49.900 --> 00:28:53.209
but 2 to 32nd power is roughly 4 billion.

00:28:53.339 --> 00:28:53.650
However,

00:28:53.739 --> 00:28:56.300
nowadays it's even more common with the Macs and PCs

00:28:56.300 --> 00:28:58.489
you might have on your laps and even your phones nowadays

00:28:58.699 --> 00:29:00.540
to use 64 bits,

00:29:00.829 --> 00:29:03.819
which is a big enough number that I'm not even sure offhand how to pronounce it.

00:29:03.859 --> 00:29:05.859
That's a lot of permutations that.

00:29:06.062 --> 00:29:08.503
2 to 64 possible permutations,

00:29:08.542 --> 00:29:10.172
but that's increasingly commonplace.

00:29:10.262 --> 00:29:10.942
And as an aside,

00:29:10.983 --> 00:29:13.042
just to dovetail things with our discussion of AI,

00:29:13.422 --> 00:29:16.022
among the reasons that we're living through over these past few years,

00:29:16.223 --> 00:29:19.262
especially this crazy interesting time of AI,

00:29:19.272 --> 00:29:19.973
is because

00:29:20.302 --> 00:29:22.642
computers have been getting so much faster,

00:29:22.772 --> 00:29:24.412
exponentially so over time.

00:29:24.623 --> 00:29:26.542
They have so much more memory available to them.

00:29:26.623 --> 00:29:29.022
There's so much data out there on the internet in particular.

00:29:29.286 --> 00:29:33.786
to train these models that it's an interesting confluence of hardware now actually

00:29:33.786 --> 00:29:37.056
meeting the mathematics and statistics that we'll talk about later in the class

00:29:37.296 --> 00:29:41.215
that ultimately make tools like the cat we just built possible.

00:29:41.385 --> 00:29:43.056
But of course computers are not all math,

00:29:43.066 --> 00:29:46.015
and in fact we'll use very little math per se in this class.

00:29:46.145 --> 00:29:48.306
And so let's move away pretty quickly from just zeros

00:29:48.306 --> 00:29:50.026
and ones and talk about letters of the alphabet,

00:29:50.105 --> 00:29:50.895
say in English.

00:29:51.066 --> 00:29:52.105
Here is the letter A.

00:29:52.869 --> 00:29:55.589
Suppose you want to use this letter in an email,

00:29:55.630 --> 00:29:56.219
a text message,

00:29:56.270 --> 00:29:57.380
or any other program.

00:29:57.550 --> 00:29:59.699
What is the computer doing underneath the hood?

00:29:59.949 --> 00:30:04.869
How can the computer store a capital letter A in English if at the end of the

00:30:04.869 --> 00:30:10.479
day all the computer has access to is a source of electricity from the wall or from

00:30:10.790 --> 00:30:11.619
a battery,

00:30:12.109 --> 00:30:15.300
and it has a lot of switches that it can turn on and off

00:30:15.300 --> 00:30:20.390
and treat the electricity in units of 8 or 32 or 64 or whatever.

00:30:21.439 --> 00:30:23.619
How might a computer represent a letter A?

00:30:26.199 --> 00:30:26.430
Yeah,

00:30:26.589 --> 00:30:27.790
we need to give it an identity,

00:30:27.869 --> 00:30:28.339
so to speak,

00:30:28.390 --> 00:30:29.319
as an integer.

00:30:29.390 --> 00:30:29.790
In other words,

00:30:29.829 --> 00:30:30.780
at the end of the day,

00:30:30.949 --> 00:30:32.790
if your entire canvas,

00:30:32.869 --> 00:30:33.579
so to speak,

00:30:33.790 --> 00:30:35.390
consists only of zeros and ones,

00:30:35.430 --> 00:30:37.859
like that is going to be the answer to every question today.

00:30:38.069 --> 00:30:41.150
You only have zeros and ones as the solution to these problems.

00:30:41.270 --> 00:30:42.420
We just need to agree

00:30:42.709 --> 00:30:45.599
what pattern of zeros and ones and therefore what integer,

00:30:45.670 --> 00:30:49.579
what number shall be used to represent the letter A.

00:30:49.750 --> 00:30:50.560
And hopefully,

00:30:50.844 --> 00:30:54.305
When we look at that pattern of zeros and ones in the right context,

00:30:54.324 --> 00:30:55.685
we'll indeed see it as an A.

00:30:55.765 --> 00:30:57.114
So if we look inside of a computer,

00:30:57.204 --> 00:30:57.834
so to speak,

00:30:58.005 --> 00:31:00.405
in the context of like a text messaging program

00:31:00.405 --> 00:31:02.635
or a word processor or anything like that,

00:31:02.844 --> 00:31:05.915
that pattern shall be interpreted hopefully as a capital letter A.

00:31:06.005 --> 00:31:09.954
But if I open up Mac OS's or Windows or my phone's calculator program,

00:31:10.244 --> 00:31:14.194
I would want that same pattern of zeros and ones to be interpreted instead

00:31:14.484 --> 00:31:14.885
as a.

00:31:15.699 --> 00:31:17.030
If I open up Photoshop,

00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:17.869
as we'll soon see,

00:31:18.020 --> 00:31:22.859
I want that same pattern of zeros and ones to be interpreted as a color presumably,

00:31:22.939 --> 00:31:24.780
not to mention videos and sound and so forth,

00:31:24.819 --> 00:31:26.500
but it's all just zeros and ones.

00:31:26.540 --> 00:31:27.619
And so even though I,

00:31:27.780 --> 00:31:30.170
when writing that chat program a few minutes ago,

00:31:30.380 --> 00:31:32.489
didn't have to worry about telling the computer,

00:31:32.660 --> 00:31:33.619
oh this is text,

00:31:33.699 --> 00:31:34.430
this is a number,

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:35.449
this is something else,

00:31:35.640 --> 00:31:38.140
we'll see as we write code ourselves that

00:31:38.140 --> 00:31:40.954
you as the programmer will Have control over telling

00:31:40.954 --> 00:31:45.395
the computer how to treat some pattern of zeros and ones telling it this is a number,

00:31:45.405 --> 00:31:46.224
this is a color,

00:31:46.354 --> 00:31:48.145
this is a letter or something else.

00:31:48.155 --> 00:31:48.165
Um,

00:31:49.155 --> 00:31:50.864
how do we represent the letter A?

00:31:50.964 --> 00:31:50.984
Well,

00:31:51.035 --> 00:31:54.795
it turns out a bunch of humans in a room years ago decided that this

00:31:54.795 --> 00:32:00.944
pattern of zeros and ones shall be known globally as a capital letter English A.

00:32:01.275 --> 00:32:03.675
What is that number if you do the quick mental math?

00:32:04.329 --> 00:32:08.939
So indeed 65 because we had a 1 in the 604s place and a 1 in the one's place.

00:32:09.020 --> 00:32:09.780
So 65,

00:32:09.819 --> 00:32:10.770
that's just sort of it.

00:32:10.900 --> 00:32:14.420
It would have been nice if it were just the number 1 or maybe the number 0,

00:32:14.500 --> 00:32:17.060
but at least after the capital letter A,

00:32:17.339 --> 00:32:19.390
they kept things consistent such that

00:32:19.739 --> 00:32:21.530
if you want to represent a letter B,

00:32:21.619 --> 00:32:23.410
it's going to be 66.

00:32:23.420 --> 00:32:23.739
C,

00:32:23.829 --> 00:32:25.250
it's going to be 67 Y because.

00:32:25.782 --> 00:32:26.802
The humans in this room,

00:32:26.853 --> 00:32:28.083
a bunch of Americans at the time,

00:32:28.213 --> 00:32:29.812
standardized on what's called ASCI,

00:32:29.853 --> 00:32:32.572
the American Standard Code for Information Interchange.

00:32:32.613 --> 00:32:34.522
It doesn't matter what the acronym represents,

00:32:34.772 --> 00:32:35.192
but

00:32:35.412 --> 00:32:36.652
it was just a mapping.

00:32:36.733 --> 00:32:38.253
Someone on a piece of paper essentially

00:32:38.253 --> 00:32:40.142
started writing down letters of the alphabet

00:32:40.333 --> 00:32:44.522
and corresponding numbers so that computers subsequently could all speak

00:32:44.522 --> 00:32:48.326
that same standard representation and Here's an excerpt thereof.

00:32:48.365 --> 00:32:49.926
In this case we're seeing 7 bits' worth,

00:32:50.005 --> 00:32:53.836
but eventually we ended up using 8 bits in total to represent letters,

00:32:53.845 --> 00:32:55.485
and some of these are fairly cryptic,

00:32:55.566 --> 00:32:56.875
maybe more on those other time,

00:32:56.926 --> 00:32:58.885
but down here if we highlight just one column,

00:32:59.245 --> 00:33:02.845
we'll see that indeed on this cheat sheet 65 is capital A,

00:33:02.916 --> 00:33:04.166
66 is B,

00:33:04.176 --> 00:33:05.355
67 is C,

00:33:05.395 --> 00:33:06.235
and so forth.

00:33:06.526 --> 00:33:08.566
So why don't we do a little exercise here.

00:33:09.069 --> 00:33:09.619
What

00:33:09.989 --> 00:33:12.510
pattern of zeros and ones do I see here?

00:33:12.550 --> 00:33:13.869
I've got 3 bytes,

00:33:14.069 --> 00:33:15.750
so 3 sets of 8 bits,

00:33:16.030 --> 00:33:18.430
and even though there's no placeholders now over the columns,

00:33:18.709 --> 00:33:19.310
what is this?

00:33:20.699 --> 00:33:21.229
Number

00:33:23.020 --> 00:33:24.640
It's 60.

00:33:25.359 --> 00:33:25.550
Yeah,

00:33:25.630 --> 00:33:26.510
so we've got the 1s,

00:33:26.560 --> 00:33:26.989
2s,

00:33:27.060 --> 00:33:27.589
4s,

00:33:27.709 --> 00:33:28.339
8s,

00:33:28.910 --> 00:33:30.660
1632,

00:33:30.869 --> 00:33:32.430
64s column.

00:33:32.550 --> 00:33:33.900
So indeed this is going to be the number

00:33:34.390 --> 00:33:35.260
72,

00:33:35.339 --> 00:33:35.829
72.

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:37.869
This is not what computer scientists spend their day doing.

00:33:37.949 --> 00:33:40.180
This is just to reinforce what it is we just looked at,

00:33:40.229 --> 00:33:40.959
and I'll spoil it.

00:33:40.969 --> 00:33:42.880
The rest of these numbers are 72,

00:33:43.069 --> 00:33:43.969
73,

00:33:43.979 --> 00:33:44.579
33,

00:33:44.589 --> 00:33:47.430
and anyone in this room could have done that if you took out a piece of paper,

00:33:47.550 --> 00:33:48.619
figured out what the columns are,

00:33:48.790 --> 00:33:49.989
and just do a bit of quick or.

00:33:50.145 --> 00:33:51.545
Of mental or written math,

00:33:51.714 --> 00:33:52.665
but this is to say,

00:33:53.005 --> 00:33:55.464
suppose that you just got a text message or an email

00:33:55.834 --> 00:33:59.314
that if you had the ability to look underneath the hood of the computer and

00:33:59.314 --> 00:34:03.185
see what pattern of zeros and ones did you just receive over the internet,

00:34:03.515 --> 00:34:07.385
suppose that pattern of zeros and ones was 3 bytes of bits,

00:34:07.675 --> 00:34:10.465
which when you do the math are the numbers 72,

00:34:10.514 --> 00:34:11.353
73,

00:34:11.364 --> 00:34:12.205
33.

00:34:12.274 --> 00:34:12.353
Well,

00:34:12.395 --> 00:34:13.274
here's the cheat sheet again.

00:34:13.353 --> 00:34:14.725
