01:02:34.360 --> 01:02:35.649
that's going to be pretty slow.

01:02:35.860 --> 01:02:37.300
But the second algorithm was.

01:02:37.437 --> 01:02:40.697
Pretty much twice as fast plus that one extra step potentially,

01:02:40.847 --> 01:02:43.937
but it's still a straight line because if there's 1000 pages

01:02:44.187 --> 01:02:47.298
and I'm dividing the problem and doing two pages at a time,

01:02:47.508 --> 01:02:47.568
well,

01:02:47.587 --> 01:02:49.907
that's like N divided by 2 steps plus 1,

01:02:50.028 --> 01:02:50.738
give or take.

01:02:50.907 --> 01:02:52.937
But it's still a straight line because,

01:02:53.187 --> 01:02:54.137
but it's still better.

01:02:54.268 --> 01:02:56.458
Notice if this is the size of the problem,

01:02:56.508 --> 01:02:58.218
1000 pages for instance,

01:02:58.387 --> 01:03:02.468
we'll notice that the first algorithm took literally twice as much time.

01:03:02.835 --> 01:03:03.795
As the second algorithm,

01:03:03.875 --> 01:03:05.266
so we're doing better already,

01:03:05.436 --> 01:03:07.466
but the third algorithm fundamentally

01:03:07.835 --> 01:03:09.135
is going to look something like this.

01:03:09.156 --> 01:03:10.645
And if you remember your logarithms,

01:03:10.795 --> 01:03:11.305
so to speak,

01:03:11.365 --> 01:03:13.065
sort of the opposite of an exponential,

01:03:13.466 --> 01:03:14.285
this curve

01:03:14.516 --> 01:03:15.466
is so much

01:03:15.756 --> 01:03:17.115
lower and flatter,

01:03:17.186 --> 01:03:17.825
if you will,

01:03:17.996 --> 01:03:19.035
than either of these two.

01:03:19.156 --> 01:03:19.746
Mathematically,

01:03:19.756 --> 01:03:20.785
more on this another time,

01:03:20.936 --> 01:03:25.426
the slope is going to be like log based 2 of N or just logarithmic in nature,

01:03:25.516 --> 01:03:27.555
but what it means is that it's growing very.

01:03:27.694 --> 01:03:28.003
Very,

01:03:28.043 --> 01:03:28.333
very,

01:03:28.483 --> 01:03:29.674
very slowly.

01:03:29.684 --> 01:03:30.364
It's still going up.

01:03:30.444 --> 01:03:32.993
It's never going to flat line and go perfectly horizontal,

01:03:33.124 --> 01:03:34.444
but it goes up very slowly.

01:03:34.523 --> 01:03:34.954
Why?

01:03:35.124 --> 01:03:35.164
Well,

01:03:35.233 --> 01:03:38.114
if you think about two towns nearby like Cambridge on this side

01:03:38.114 --> 01:03:40.313
of the river and the town of Alston on the other,

01:03:40.684 --> 01:03:43.313
suppose that they still have phone books like this one and they merge

01:03:43.563 --> 01:03:45.114
their phone books for whatever reason.

01:03:45.204 --> 01:03:49.483
So overnight we go from a 1000 page phone book to a 2000 page phone book.

01:03:49.684 --> 01:03:52.513
The first algorithm is going to take literally twice as long.

01:03:52.822 --> 01:03:56.642
As well the second one because we're only going through it 1 or 2 pages at a time,

01:03:56.822 --> 01:03:58.612
but if the phone book size doubles

01:03:59.052 --> 01:03:59.842
from this year,

01:04:00.132 --> 01:04:00.471
for instance,

01:04:00.531 --> 01:04:01.402
to next year,

01:04:01.652 --> 01:04:04.362
you can kind of in your mind's eye think about the green line.

01:04:04.562 --> 01:04:05.521
It's not going to go up

01:04:05.691 --> 01:04:07.322
that much higher.

01:04:07.612 --> 01:04:08.082
Why?

01:04:08.251 --> 01:04:08.372
Well,

01:04:08.451 --> 01:04:09.362
practically speaking,

01:04:09.511 --> 01:04:13.041
even if the phone book becomes 2000 pages long,

01:04:13.332 --> 01:04:13.491
well,

01:04:13.612 --> 01:04:17.711
how many more times do you have to tear or divide that problem in half?

01:04:18.889 --> 01:04:23.489
Just one because you're taking a 1000 page bye out of it or a 500 then a 250,

01:04:23.510 --> 01:04:27.959
you're taking much bigger bytes out of it than just one or two at a time.

01:04:28.090 --> 01:04:31.090
And so what computer science and what algorithms and about

01:04:31.090 --> 01:04:34.459
good design is about is figuring out what is the logic

01:04:34.689 --> 01:04:39.679
via which you can solve problems not only correctly but efficiently as well.

01:04:39.929 --> 01:04:42.370
And that then gives us these things called algorithms.

01:04:42.399 --> 01:04:43.560
And when it comes time to code,

01:04:43.610 --> 01:04:44.729
which we're about to do too.

01:04:45.340 --> 01:04:48.850
Code is just an implementation and a language the computer understands

01:04:49.179 --> 01:04:50.100
of an algorithm.

01:04:50.300 --> 01:04:52.850
Now this assumes that we've come up with some digital way,

01:04:52.939 --> 01:04:56.649
that is to say 0 and 1 based way to represent names

01:04:56.860 --> 01:04:57.530
and numbers,

01:04:57.540 --> 01:04:58.750
but honestly we already did that.

01:04:58.780 --> 01:05:02.100
We came up with the ASCI and then Unicode to represent the names,

01:05:02.179 --> 01:05:04.260
and representing numbers is even easier than that.

01:05:04.360 --> 01:05:05.770
That's really where we started.

01:05:06.100 --> 01:05:07.820
So code is just about taking it.

01:05:07.972 --> 01:05:11.643
Input some standardized representation of names and numbers and spitting out

01:05:11.643 --> 01:05:15.113
answers and that's truly what iOS and Android are doing.

01:05:15.282 --> 01:05:16.913
When you start doing autocomplete,

01:05:17.232 --> 01:05:20.272
they could be searching from the top to the bottom,

01:05:20.443 --> 01:05:23.472
which is fine if you've only got a few friends and family in the phone.

01:05:23.643 --> 01:05:27.893
But if you've got 1000 or if you've got 10,000 or if it's not a phone book anymore,

01:05:27.962 --> 01:05:30.232
it's some database with lots and lots of data.

01:05:30.403 --> 01:05:30.722
Well it's.

01:05:30.986 --> 01:05:34.186
to reason that it'd be nice maybe if the computer kept it

01:05:34.186 --> 01:05:37.615
all alphabetized just like that book and jumped to the middle,

01:05:37.865 --> 01:05:38.865
then the middle of the middle,

01:05:38.916 --> 01:05:41.016
then the middle of the middle of the middle and so forth.

01:05:41.145 --> 01:05:41.496
Why?

01:05:41.906 --> 01:05:44.656
Because the speed is going to be much,

01:05:44.706 --> 01:05:45.295
much faster,

01:05:45.466 --> 01:05:47.976
logarithmic in nature and not linear,

01:05:48.145 --> 01:05:48.545
so to speak,

01:05:48.555 --> 01:05:48.976
in nature,

01:05:49.025 --> 01:05:50.855
but we'll revisit those topics as well.

01:05:51.025 --> 01:05:51.775
But for now,

01:05:52.065 --> 01:05:53.656
before we get into actual code.

01:05:54.149 --> 01:05:55.469
Let's talk for a moment about

01:05:55.830 --> 01:05:56.510
pseudo code.

01:05:56.550 --> 01:05:58.439
So pseudo code is not one formal thing,

01:05:58.629 --> 01:06:02.060
but every human will come up with their own way of representing pseudo code.

01:06:02.229 --> 01:06:05.169
It's an English-like or human-like formulation of

01:06:05.169 --> 01:06:07.739
step by step instructions just using terse

01:06:08.270 --> 01:06:08.699
correct

01:06:09.030 --> 01:06:10.620
English or whatever human language.

01:06:10.790 --> 01:06:11.540
So for instance,

01:06:11.629 --> 01:06:14.379
if I want to translate what I did somewhat intuitively

01:06:14.379 --> 01:06:16.189
with that phone book by just dividing in half,

01:06:16.229 --> 01:06:18.320
dividing in half into step by step instructions,

01:06:18.389 --> 01:06:18.870
I could hand.

01:06:19.405 --> 01:06:22.155
Or nowadays like a robot or something like that.

01:06:22.324 --> 01:06:22.405
Well,

01:06:22.524 --> 01:06:24.245
step one was essentially to pick up the phone book,

01:06:24.364 --> 01:06:25.074
which I did.

01:06:25.364 --> 01:06:29.465
Step 2 was I opened to the middle of the phone book in the third and final algorithm.

01:06:29.645 --> 01:06:31.995
Step 3 was look at the page as I did.

01:06:32.205 --> 01:06:35.364
Step 4 got a little more interesting even though I didn't verbalize this.

01:06:35.514 --> 01:06:37.554
Presumably I was asking myself a question.

01:06:37.804 --> 01:06:39.054
If the person I'm looking for,

01:06:39.205 --> 01:06:39.725
John Harvard,

01:06:39.764 --> 01:06:40.875
is on the page,

01:06:41.324 --> 01:06:41.975
then

01:06:42.324 --> 01:06:43.844
I would have called him right then.

01:06:44.350 --> 01:06:46.120
But if you weren't on the page,

01:06:46.330 --> 01:06:48.260
if you instead were earlier in the book,

01:06:48.449 --> 01:06:49.409
as did happen,

01:06:49.610 --> 01:06:51.090
well then I'm going to go to the left,

01:06:51.169 --> 01:06:51.719
so to speak,

01:06:51.770 --> 01:06:53.050
but more methodically,

01:06:53.169 --> 01:06:55.840
I'm going to open to the middle of the left half of the book.

01:06:56.129 --> 01:06:57.840
Then I'm going to go back to

01:06:58.449 --> 01:06:59.600
line 3.

01:06:59.770 --> 01:07:00.290
That's interesting.

01:07:00.330 --> 01:07:01.439
We'll come back to that in a moment,

01:07:01.570 --> 01:07:03.600
but else if the person is later in the book,

01:07:03.689 --> 01:07:03.729
well,

01:07:03.810 --> 01:07:06.540
I'm going to open to the middle of the right half of the book

01:07:06.889 --> 01:07:08.520
and then go back

01:07:08.729 --> 01:07:09.939
to line 3.

01:07:10.280 --> 01:07:10.469
Now,

01:07:10.479 --> 01:07:11.429
let's pause here.

01:07:11.600 --> 01:07:13.199
Why do I keep going back to line 3?

01:07:13.280 --> 01:07:15.510
This would seem to get me doing the same thing

01:07:16.120 --> 01:07:16.949
forever,

01:07:17.320 --> 01:07:18.080
endlessly.

01:07:19.280 --> 01:07:20.120
But not quite.

01:07:23.760 --> 01:07:23.770
Why?

01:07:24.439 --> 01:07:24.870
Yeah,

01:07:24.989 --> 01:07:28.550
so because I am dividing the problem in half,

01:07:28.790 --> 01:07:29.189
for instance,

01:07:29.270 --> 01:07:33.100
on line 6 or line 9 implicitly just based on how I've written this,

01:07:33.149 --> 01:07:35.560
the problem's getting smaller and smaller and smaller.

01:07:35.790 --> 01:07:37.909
So it's fine if I keep doing the same logic

01:07:37.909 --> 01:07:39.800
again and again because if the problem's getting smaller.

01:07:40.090 --> 01:07:42.439
Eventually it's going to bottom out and I'm gonna have just

01:07:42.739 --> 01:07:46.760
one person on that page that I want to call and so the algorithm's done.

01:07:46.889 --> 01:07:48.959
But there is a perverse corner case,

01:07:48.969 --> 01:07:49.399
if you will,

01:07:49.489 --> 01:07:51.770
and this is where it's ever more important to be

01:07:51.770 --> 01:07:55.350
precise when writing code and anticipate what could go wrong.

01:07:55.810 --> 01:07:58.679
I should probably ask one more question in this code,

01:07:58.770 --> 01:07:59.889
not just these three.

01:08:00.879 --> 01:08:01.760
What might that

01:08:01.979 --> 01:08:02.639
question.

01:08:04.100 --> 01:08:06.320
Yeah.

01:08:07.520 --> 01:08:07.790
Yeah,

01:08:07.909 --> 01:08:09.739
so if John Harvard is not in the book,

01:08:09.790 --> 01:08:13.070
there's this corner case where what if I'm just wasting my time entirely and

01:08:13.070 --> 01:08:15.179
I get to the end of the phone book and John Harvard's not there,

01:08:15.350 --> 01:08:16.580
what should the computer do?

01:08:16.750 --> 01:08:16.790
Well,

01:08:16.830 --> 01:08:17.350
as an aside,

01:08:17.390 --> 01:08:20.750
if you've ever been using your Mac or PC or phone and the thing just freezes

01:08:20.750 --> 01:08:22.390
or like the stupid little beach ball starts

01:08:22.390 --> 01:08:23.819
spinning or something like that and you're like,

01:08:23.830 --> 01:08:24.830
what is going on?

01:08:25.149 --> 01:08:29.339
Some human at Google or Microsoft or Apple or the like made a mistake.

01:08:29.509 --> 01:08:30.140
They forgot,

01:08:30.149 --> 01:08:30.700
for instance,

01:08:30.709 --> 01:08:33.669
that 4th uncommon but possible.

01:08:33.743 --> 01:08:36.844
Situation wherein if they don't tell the computer how to handle it,

01:08:37.002 --> 01:08:39.724
the computer's effectively going to freak out and do something

01:08:39.724 --> 01:08:43.733
undefined like just hang or reboot or do something else.

01:08:43.854 --> 01:08:45.283
So we do want to add this else

01:08:46.252 --> 01:08:47.663
quit altogether.

01:08:47.764 --> 01:08:50.934
So you have well defined behavior and truly think that the next time

01:08:50.934 --> 01:08:55.323
your computer or phone spontaneously reboots or dies or does something wrong,

01:08:55.493 --> 01:08:57.413
it's probably not your fault per se.

01:08:57.502 --> 01:08:59.854
It's some other human elsewhere did not.

01:09:00.240 --> 01:09:03.870
Correct code they didn't anticipate cases like these.

01:09:04.080 --> 01:09:05.680
But now let's use some terminology here.

01:09:05.839 --> 01:09:08.839
There's some salient ideas that we're going to see in scratch and

01:09:08.839 --> 01:09:11.709
C and Python and these other languages I alluded to earlier.

01:09:11.959 --> 01:09:13.560
Everything I've just highlighted here

01:09:13.959 --> 01:09:16.200
henceforth we're gonna think of as functions.

01:09:16.319 --> 01:09:21.430
Functions are verbs or actions that really get some small piece of work done for you.

01:09:21.680 --> 01:09:23.589
Functions are verbs or actions.

01:09:23.899 --> 01:09:26.040
Here though highlighted is the beginning of what we'll call.

01:09:26.194 --> 01:09:27.104
Conditions,

01:09:27.424 --> 01:09:28.904
conditionals like a fork in the road.

01:09:28.995 --> 01:09:29.895
Do I go this way?

01:09:30.064 --> 01:09:30.785
Do I go this way,

01:09:30.865 --> 01:09:32.214
or some other way altogether?

01:09:32.384 --> 01:09:34.935
How do you decide what road to go down?

01:09:35.185 --> 01:09:37.615
We're going to call these questions you ask yourself

01:09:37.825 --> 01:09:39.415
Boolean expressions,

01:09:39.464 --> 01:09:40.785
named after a mathematician Bull,

01:09:40.984 --> 01:09:44.384
and a boolean expression is just a question that has a yes or no

01:09:44.384 --> 01:09:49.214
answer or a true or false answer or a 1 or zero answer.

01:09:49.265 --> 01:09:50.745
Just it's a binary state,

01:09:50.865 --> 01:09:52.305
yes or no typically.

01:09:52.979 --> 01:09:55.939
Otherwise we have this go back to go back to,

01:09:56.020 --> 01:09:57.169
which is what we're generally kind of

01:09:57.169 --> 01:10:00.049
call a loop which somehow induces cyclical behavior

01:10:00.490 --> 01:10:01.509
again and again

01:10:01.729 --> 01:10:03.870
and those functions and those conditionals,

01:10:03.879 --> 01:10:06.740
boolean expressions and loops and a few other concepts are pretty

